---
/**
 * Commercial license summary for the marketing landing.
 *
 * IMPORTANT — this is a *summary* of the license, not the legally
 * executed EULA. The full text of the LICENSE file in the repo is
 * what governs the actual sale; this page exists so prospective buyers
 * can read the gist without cloning the repo.
 *
 * Keep the tier perks in step with `src/config/site.ts` and the FAQ —
 * a buyer who reads two different lists will believe the more generous
 * one. Have the executed EULA reviewed by counsel before launch.
 */
import Legal from "@/layouts/legal.astro"
import { site } from "@/config/site"

const lastUpdated = "2026-09-03"
const contactEmail = site.social.email
---

<Legal
  title="License"
  description="A plain-English summary of the LaunchKit commercial license."
  eyebrow={`Effective · ${lastUpdated}`}
>
  <p>
    {site.brand} is sold under a <strong>commercial license</strong>: you buy
    once, you ship as many products as you want, you don't redistribute the
    boilerplate itself. The full executed EULA ships inside each repository as
    <code>LICENSE</code>; this page is the short version for prospective buyers.
  </p>

  <h2>You can</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Use the source code to build <strong>commercial applications</strong> — paid
      SaaS, internal tools, side projects. How many depends on your tier:
      <strong>Solo</strong> licenses one product, <strong>Unlimited</strong>
      licenses as many as you want and adds work you build for clients.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Modify</strong> the code freely — rename files, swap stacks, delete
      features, brand it as your own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deploy</strong> compiled and source forms of your applications to any
      host, on any number of servers.
    </li>
    <li>
      Use it as the foundation for client deliverables. The license is tied to
      you; your client gets the application you build, not the boilerplate
      license itself.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>You cannot</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Resell or redistribute the boilerplate</strong> as a starter kit, template,
      code generator, or competing product.
    </li>
    <li>
      Publish, sublicense, or share the source code as-is to third parties —
      except as bundled inside an application you built.
    </li>
    <li>
      Share your access to the private GitHub organization, or transfer your
      license to a third party without our written consent.
    </li>
    <li>
      Ship more products than your tier licenses. There is no technical gate on
      this — the repositories are identical on both tiers — so it runs on good
      faith. If your second product is doing well, upgrade; we'll credit what
      you already paid.
    </li>
    <li>
      Remove the proprietary notices contained in the codebase in any
      redistribution. (Inside your own applications you can replace them with
      your own.)
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Updates and support</h2>
  <p>
    Both tiers ship the same source code, get the same repository access, and
    receive <strong>updates for as long as {site.brand} is maintained</strong>.
    There is no renewal and nothing expires. What differs:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Solo</strong> — one licensed product, plus email support.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Unlimited</strong> — unlimited licensed products, client work, email
      support, the private Discord, early access to new features, and a vote on the
      roadmap.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Security fixes ship to <strong>all license holders</strong> for free, regardless
    of tier. If you outgrow Solo, email us and we'll credit what you paid against
    the difference rather than charging you twice.
  </p>

  <h2>Refunds</h2>
  <p>
    Access is delivered through a claim link that arrives within minutes of
    checkout. Once you've claimed it and been added to the private GitHub
    organization, the source code is in your hands — so we don't offer refunds
    after that point. If you change your mind <em>before</em> claiming access, email
    us within <strong>7 days</strong> of purchase and we'll refund in full.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fairest way to avoid buyer's regret is to read the full FAQ and email us
    with any questions <em>before</em> you check out. We're happy to walk you through
    specific parts of the codebase, share a Loom of the dashboard, or answer anything
    that's still unclear. We'd rather lose a sale than have you regret it.
  </p>

  <h2>Warranty and liability</h2>
  <p>
    The software is provided <em>as is</em>, without warranty of any kind,
    express or implied. To the maximum extent permitted by law, our total
    liability under this license is limited to the fees you paid for it. The
    full disclaimer is in the executed <code>LICENSE</code> file shipped with the
    codebase.
  </p>

  <h2>Third-party dependencies</h2>
  <p>
    The codebase includes third-party open source software, each governed by its
    own license. You're responsible for complying with the terms of those
    components. The headline ones (Django, Django REST Framework, Next.js,
    React, Tailwind, shadcn/ui) are MIT or BSD-licensed and impose no additional
    restrictions on you.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    For licensing questions or enterprise terms, email{" "}
    <a href={`mailto:${contactEmail}`}>{contactEmail}</a>.
  </p>
</Legal>
